    "messages": [
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "The deployment config-reader in namespace scenario-test cannot start. The team confirms the ConfigMap app-config exists and contains the application configuration. Investigate why the pod is failing to launch."
      }
    ],
    "final_text": "INVESTIGATION_PLAN:\n1. Describe the pod `config-reader-d947cbfb7-cplvd` in the `scenario-test` namespace to inspect its events and confirm the exact issue with the volume mount.\n2. Get the ConfigMap `app-config` in the `scenario-test` namespace to verify its contents and ensure the key `app.conf` exists.\n3. Check the pod spec to confirm how the ConfigMap is referenced and whether the key `app.conf` is correctly specified.\n\nProceeding with parallel tool calls.### Findings:\n1. **Pod Description**:\n   - The pod `config-reader-d947cbfb7-cplvd` in the `scenario-test` namespace is stuck in `ContainerCreating` state.\n   - The event log indicates a `FailedMount` error: \"MountVolume.SetUp failed for volume `config-vol`: configmap references non-existent config key: `app.conf`\".\n   - The pod spec attempts to mount the key `app.conf` from the ConfigMap `app-config` into `/etc/config/app.conf`.\n\n2. **ConfigMap Content**:\n   - The ConfigMap `app-config` exists in the `scenario-test` namespace.\n   - However, it does not contain the key `app.conf`. Instead, it contains:\n     - `app.properties`\n     - `feature-flags.yaml`\n\n### Root Cause:\nThe pod spec references a non-existent key `app.conf` in the ConfigMap `app-config`. This mismatch prevents the volume mount from succeeding.\n\n### Suggested Fix:\nUpdate the pod spec to reference an existing key in the ConfigMap, such as `app.properties` or `feature-flags.yaml`. Alternatively, if the key `app.conf` is required, it must be added to the ConfigMap.\n\nWould you like me to:\n1. Update the pod spec to use `app.properties`?\n2. Add the key `app.conf` to the ConfigMap with appropriate content?",
